---
# Catalog usage prompt for your GenUI app.
#
# This dotprompt asset describes the relationships between the components in this
# catalog — when to use each one, how they compose — so the model picks the
# right component for the job. It is the middle layer between the A2UI mechanics
# prompt (how to emit A2UI JSON, injected by the server) and the system prompt
# (your app's voice and audience). Keep it about component selection: do not
# restate A2UI/JSON mechanics here, and do not write voice or audience here.
#
# It lives in the shared catalog package so it travels with the components it
# describes. The package exports its location as `catalogPromptAssetUri`; the
# API server resolves that, renders this asset once at startup, and prepends it
# as a system message after the A2UI instructions.
#
# It ships empty, so by default no system message is sent for it. Write the body
# below the closing delimiter to describe your catalog, or run the
# `build_genui_catalog_prompt` MCP prompt to generate it from your components.
#
# These Handlebars variables are always available in the body:
#   catalogId       - the catalog id the app renders against
#   componentNames  - backtick-wrapped, comma-separated component names
# Handlebars escapes values by default; use triple braces for raw injection.
# Do not put secrets here; this file is version-controlled.
---

Catalog `{{catalogId}}` renders the A2UI VGV Smart Home panel for a landscape kitchen
wall tablet. Available components: {{{componentNames}}}.

## Choosing what leads

Decide which cards the screen shows before you lay anything out; the layout
prompt says where they go and how big each one is. Call `get_home_activity`
first and rank the candidates in this order:

1. What just happened (`recent`, newest first). A request the user just made
   (`kind: change`) or something that changed on its own (the oven reached its
   temperature, a delivery arrived, the doorbell rang). It leads, and the card
   for it carries a tag (`updated`, `moved`, `new`) or is the `ChangeSummary`.
2. What is urgent: a moment in `next` with `urgent: true` (for example the
   shopping list due before the cooking starts) ranks with what just happened,
   above the rest of `next`. Show the card it names (`listId` is a
   `ShoppingList`), with every item of the list, ticked or not.
3. What is next (`next`, soonest first). The card that answers the next step or
   event of the next hour: the plan, the oven, the trip.
4. The person's anchor: what their role cares about most (the host's dinner
   countdown and plan; the logistics person's trip and car).
5. Everything else, to fill the grid. A device with nothing going on goes
   first when you run out of room.

Fill the cards from the data, not from memory. Entries, times and labels are
copied from the tool results exactly as they are: a plan lists only the steps
and events still ahead, never one that is already over, in order, up to what
its cell shows, each with the `time`,
`title`, `detail` and `icon` the step has, a date as `subtitle`, and the time
now as its `tag`.

You never write an action's name, context or label. Controls say what they act
on with typed props (`deviceId`, `sceneId`, `listId`, item `id`s) and the app
builds the press from them. Use ids exactly as the tools return them
(`get_google_home_data`, `get_todo_lists`); never invent or rename one. A press on an
id that does not exist fails.

A chip or a heads-up button carries one typed `action`, exactly one of:
`{"command": {"deviceId": "oven", "command": "start"}}` (one command on one
device, from `on`, `off`, `start`, `stop`, `open`, `close`, `lock`, `unlock`,
`add_time`, `set_level`, `set_temperature`, `up`, `down`),
`{"scene": "<sceneId>"}`, `{"undo": true}`, or `{"ask": "Tell guests dinner is at
8:00"}`. Use `ask` for anything that is not one device command or a scene
(telling someone, replying, several devices at once, anything that needs
judgment); the text is sent to you as if the user had typed it. The chip's or
button's visible `label` is what the user sees while it runs, so make it an
imperative phrase that reads on its own ("Preheat oven"). Never offer a
command whose result is already the device's state in the data you read this
turn (lock a door that is `locked`, switch on what is `on`, preheat an oven
that is preheating): pick the next step that still changes something, or no
chip.

- `CountdownCard`: one upcoming calendar event with a big countdown, for when a
  single event dominates the next few hours (dinner party, pickup). Send
  `startsAt` as ISO 8601 with offset; never send the countdown, the client
  computes and ticks it. When the event moves, re-send it with the new
  `startsAt` and `subtitle`. Default tone `lighting`.
- `PlanTimeline`: the ordered plan for the evening, 1 to 5 entries in time
  order. Use it when there is more than one dated thing today or the plan just
  changed; for a single event use `CountdownCard`. `density: detailed` (the
  default) has icon bubbles and a detail line and is the tall card;
  `density: compact` shows times only and is the half tall one. When entries move, set
  `previousTime` on them (and `changedBy` when someone else moved them), add
  an `updated` tag, and mark the others `status: unchanged` when shown to a
  second person.
- `DeviceCard`: one device's state (oven, fridge, thermostat, lights, blinds,
  lock, coffee maker, weather). `deviceId` is required and is the device's id.
  Icon, `name`, `status`, a hero `value` with small `unit`, optional `delta`.
  One `control`: `toggle`, `stepper`, `button` (needs a `label` and the
  `command` it runs) or `none`; the app knows what each does for that device.
  Pick `tone` by device: oven, fridge, speaker, timer, coffee neutral; lights
  and blinds lighting; thermostat climate; energy meter and charger energy;
  locks, garage and doorbell security; outside weather weather. Send every prop
  as a literal value read from the device data; do not use data-model bindings.
  What `isActive` and a toggle's `on` mean follows the device: lit, running,
  open (blinds, garage door), locked (locks), armed, or charging. After a press,
  show the new state without a `tag`. A `tag` ("Updated just now") sits beside
  the icon and marks a device that changed on its own; the control stays. Set
  `isLoading` only on a device whose command is still running. Never use a red
  `DeviceCard` for a warning; use a `HeadsUpCard`.
- `ActionChips`: the bottom "Suggested" row, 1 to 3 chips. Each is a verb-first
  next step of 22 characters or fewer tied to the person, the plan and the
  device state, and re-emitted after a plan change ("Tell guests 8:00", "Undo
  change"). The first chip is `isPrimary`. Each chip has a typed `action`.
- `TripCard`: where the user must be and when to leave: `leaveBy` plus up to 3
  stops, each with an `icon` (`school`, `cake`, `home`). After a delay, send `previousLeaveBy`, an
  `updated` tag and a `footer` with tone `warning` and icon `traffic`. A stop
  with slack gets `emphasis: good`. For something under way, such as a delivery
  out for delivery, add `progress` (`percent` 0 to 100 and an `icon` such as
  `delivery`): the card gets a track with the icon at that point, shows the
  last two stops (where it left, where it is going) and drops `previousLeaveBy`. Plans and pickups come from Calendar and
  Maps, not Home devices.
- `ChargeCard`: the car, always energy tone. Send `percent`,
  `range`, `summary`, and a `window` with `windowIcon` `clock` (a window) or
  `savings` (a saving). Below 30% the bar turns warm, so also add an
  `HeadsUpCard`.
- `SceneCard`: a named group of device settings run with one tap
  (`sceneId` is required). `state: ready` shows Start; `running` shows an On
  tag and no button. Up to 4 `settings`. Default tone `lighting`.
- `HeadsUpCard`: a timely item (traffic, rain, a hot room), with a `source`, a headline `title`, a one-line `message` that gives the
  reason, a `timestamp` and up to 2 `actions`; the first is primary. The
  `variant` is `headsUp` (amber, the default) or `urgent` (red, only for
  something to act on now, such as an open fridge door). Where the layout allows only
  one action, put the other in `ActionChips`. Each action has
  a `label` and a typed `action`. When the server finishes an action, re-send
  the card with that action `state: done` and a past-tense label ("Blinds
  closed").
- `ChangeSummary`: confirms what a request changed, only right after it, at most
  one. It is the highlight of that screen and it leads. An `eyebrow` ("Done · from your request at 5:45 PM"), a
  `title` ("Dinner moved to 8:00 PM") and 1 to 4 `changes`, each with `icon`,
  `label`, `value` and either `previous` ("was 6:00 PM") or `note`. It has no
  buttons; pair it with `ActionChips` such as "Tell guests 8:00" and "Undo
  change".
- `ShoppingList`: a checklist of 2 to 5 items (never fewer than two), such as shopping for tonight. Send every item of the list, ticked or not: ticked ones stay, with `items[].checked` as a literal boolean, so the person sees the whole list and what is left.
  `listId` is required and each item `id` is the item's id from the list.
  Ticking flips it at once. `footerAction` is optional: a `label` and `icon`
  that share the list.

Keep each screen to what one person needs right now, and let two people on the
same panel get visibly different layouts.
